<Guide slug="how-pawn-loans-work-in-georgia">
  <p>
    A pawn loan is one of the simplest ways to borrow money. You leave something you own with a licensed pawnbroker, get cash on the
    spot, and pick the item back up when you repay. There's no credit check, because the item is the security for the loan.
  </p>

  <h2>What you bring</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The item.</strong> Jewelry, gold, tools, electronics, firearms, instruments: anything with resale value.</li>
    <li><strong>A valid photo ID.</strong> Georgia pawnbrokers must record who they lend to, so a government-issued photo ID is required.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessories.</strong> Chargers, cases, batteries, and original boxes make an item easier to value and often worth more.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the amount is set</h2>
  <p>
    The pawnbroker looks at what the item would sell for today, its condition, and how quickly it sells. The loan is a portion of that
    value. Gold and silver are mostly priced on their metal content; see{' '}
    <a href="/guides/how-gold-is-priced-when-you-sell/">how gold is priced</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The pawn ticket</h2>
  <p>
    Georgia law requires every pawn loan to be written down on a pawn ticket that you sign. The ticket is your receipt and your
    contract. It shows:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A description of your item</li>
    <li>The amount you borrowed</li>
    <li>The charges for the loan</li>
    <li>The date the loan is due, and the total you'll pay to get the item back</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Georgia also limits what a pawnbroker may charge. Read the ticket before you sign, ask about anything that isn't clear, and keep
    it somewhere safe: you'll need it to pick up your item.
  </p>

  <h2>Repaying and picking up your item</h2>
  <p>
    Bring your ticket and ID before the due date and pay the amount shown. Your item comes back to you in the same condition you left
    it. You can usually repay early, too.
  </p>

  <h2>Extending your loan</h2>
  <p>
    If you need more time, you can usually extend the loan by paying the charges due. The loan then runs for another period. Call the
    shop before your due date to find out exactly what's needed.
  </p>

  <h2>If you don't repay</h2>
  <p>
    If you don't repay or extend by the end of the loan, the item becomes the pawnbroker's property and the loan is closed. That's the
    whole consequence: there are no collection calls and nothing goes on your credit report. Many people choose this on purpose
    instead of selling outright, because it leaves the door open to get the item back.
  </p>

  <h2>Pawn or sell?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pawn</strong> if you want the item back and only need cash for a short time. <strong>Sell</strong> if you don't need it
    anymore; selling usually gets you more money up front, because there's nothing to repay.
  </p>
</Guide>
